Lab 02 — TensorFlow Lite: Training and Edge Deployment
Course: ITAI 3377
Date: January 28, 2026
Tool: Google Colab

What This Lab Was About
This lab was about taking a trained model and getting it ready to run on a small device. I used the MNIST dataset — handwritten digits from 0 to 9 — trained a simple neural network on it, then converted it to TensorFlow Lite format and ran it using the TFLite interpreter.
The whole point was to simulate what actually happens when AI gets deployed on edge hardware like a phone or a Raspberry Pi.

What I Did

Checked that Python and TensorFlow were installed
Loaded the MNIST dataset and normalized the pixel values from 0–255 down to 0–1
Built a simple model: Flatten → Dense(128, ReLU) → Dense(10, Softmax)
Trained for 5 epochs — accuracy got to about 98% by the end
Saved the model as mnist_model.h5
Converted it to mnist_model.tflite using TFLiteConverter
Ran the TFLite model on a test image — predicted 7, actual label was 7


What I Learned
Before this lab I thought about AI mostly in terms of training. I didn't really think about what happens after. TensorFlow Lite showed me that a trained model has to be converted into a smaller, faster format before it can run on a device with limited memory and no GPU.
The difference between TensorFlow and TensorFlow Lite is basically: TensorFlow is for building and training, TFLite is for running on hardware that can't handle the full version.

What Was Hard
Getting the TFLite inference steps right took some trial and error. You have to allocate tensors, set the input, invoke the model, then read the output — and the input data has to be exactly the right shape and type (float32). It wasn't obvious at first why the model would fail silently if the input was wrong.

